\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Threat-band layout for the Federated Harbor. The Anchor paper's defense-in-depth structure carries over: every claim either has a mechanization artifact (teal), a named bound that the bond can price against (amber), or an explicit acknowledgement of the open frontier (cobalt). The paper is disciplined about which band each claim lives in --- see Appendix~\ref {app:fh-mech} for the artifact registry. Reading left to right runs deeper into threat space and out of formal reach; bands shift left over time as future work mechanizes what is presently bounded.}}{12}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fh-threat-bands}{{2}{12}{Threat-band layout for the Federated Harbor. The Anchor paper's defense-in-depth structure carries over: every claim either has a mechanization artifact (teal), a named bound that the bond can price against (amber), or an explicit acknowledgement of the open frontier (cobalt). The paper is disciplined about which band each claim lives in --- see Appendix~\ref {app:fh-mech} for the artifact registry. Reading left to right runs deeper into threat space and out of formal reach; bands shift left over time as future work mechanizes what is presently bounded}{figure.caption.16}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4}Cross-Harbor Capability Transfer}{12}{section.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:fh-xfer}{{4}{12}{Cross-Harbor Capability Transfer}{section.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The four-message cross-harbor capability transfer ceremony. The critical property is that no message after (3) needs to reach back to Harbor~$A$ in the hot path: $C_B'$ is verifiable under $\mathit  {sk}_B$ alone, which is why federation does not turn every authorization into a synchronous inter-machine round trip. The epoch root $R_A^{(e)}$ bound into the envelope is the load-bearing primitive — a revocation issued by $A$ after epoch $e$ invalidates $C_B'$ as soon as the new $R_A^{(e+1)}$ reaches the witness log that $B$ audits.}}{13}{figure.caption.17}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fh-xfer}{{3}{13}{The four-message cross-harbor capability transfer ceremony. The critical property is that no message after (3) needs to reach back to Harbor~$A$ in the hot path: $C_B'$ is verifiable under $\mathit {sk}_B$ alone, which is why federation does not turn every authorization into a synchronous inter-machine round trip. The epoch root $R_A^{(e)}$ bound into the envelope is the load-bearing primitive — a revocation issued by $A$ after epoch $e$ invalidates $C_B'$ as soon as the new $R_A^{(e+1)}$ reaches the witness log that $B$ audits}{figure.caption.17}{}}
